urns, post-surgical recovery, and in critically ill patients. It plays a crucial role in various metabolic processes, including nitrogen transport, immune response modulation, and serving as an energy source for enterocytes and intestinal cells. \n\nThis amino acid is often added to enteral diets to support gut health and is suggested to improve glycemic control in diabetic patients when supplemented in nutritional therapy. In the context of critical care, glutamine may be supplemented to manage nutritional needs, particularly in patients with burns or those undergoing chemotherapy and surgery, as it has been shown to help prevent nutritional problems and improve postoperative complication rates.\n\nResearch has indicated that glutamine can enhance intestinal barrier function, improve insulin sensitivity, and reduce hospital complications. It is also recognized for its potential benefits in patients with malabsorption, as it may aid in the absorption of water and electrolytes. However, the recommendations for its use in parenteral nutrition are not uniform across studies, and while it has been previously recommended for routine use, recent studies have raised questions about its efficacy.\n\nIn specific medical contexts, such as hematopoietic stem cell transplantation, glutamine has demonstrated beneficial effects, and it has been speculated to help alleviate symptoms of hepatic veno-occlusive disease in transplant patients. Additionally, glutamine is noted for its role as an immunomodulator, which can optimize recovery post-exercise and protect against mucositis during chemotherapy and radiotherapy treatments.\n\nDespite its potential benefits, the use of glutamine in clinical practice is sometimes questioned due to inconclusive evidence regarding its effectiveness, and it is not included in standard parenteral nutrition solutions due to its instability. Nonetheless, it remains a significant nutrient in the nutritional management of various patient populations, particularly those experiencing metabolic stress or undergoing intensive medical treatments.\",34\r\n1888,NUCLEÓTIDOS,\"NUCLEÓTIDOS are compounds included in enteral diets that support cellular function and immune response. They are classified as immunonutrients and serve as precursors of RNA and DNA, which enhances T lymphocyte function.\",2\r\n4160,RIÑÓN,\"Riñón, also known as the kidney, is a vital organ that plays several important roles in the body. It serves as a donor of glutamine during periods of stress, thereby enhancing the availability of this amino acid. Additionally, the kidney is implicated in various metabolic disorders, and in certain cases of glucogenosis, renal transplantation may be necessary. Furthermore, the kidney is involved in glucose metabolism and can be affected by insulin resistance, which contributes to the development of metabolic syndrome. Overall, Riñón is essential for maintaining metabolic balance and responding to physiological challenges.\",3\r\n5715,TAMOXIFENO,\"Tamoxifen, also known as Tamoxifeno, is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) that is primarily used in the treatment and prevention of breast cancer. In addition to its role in oncology, Tamoxifen has been noted for its effects on bone density, making it a multifaceted medication in the management of breast cancer and related health concerns.\",3\r\n4976,METOTREXATO,\"Methotrexate, known as Metotrexato, is a medication that serves as a chemotherapy agent and immune system suppressant, primarily used in the treatment of cancer and autoimmune diseases. Its mechanism of action involves the inhibition of folate reductase, which can lead to a deficiency of folic acid and may contribute to anemia, particularly in patients with inflammatory bowel disease. Additionally, Methotrexate is classified as a cytotoxic agent that interacts pharmacokinetically with glutamine, potentially enhancing its intratumoral concentrations and therapeutic effects.\",6\r\n13809,ADMINISTRACIÓN